'''Axarquía''' (from {{W|Arabic language|Bayani}} شرقية‏  ''Šarqiyya'', {{Literal translation|eastern region}}), is the historic capital and most-populous city of the [[Araucaria|Araucarian community]], and the [[List of cities in Balisca by population|fifth-most-populous in Balisca]] overall. In 2022, the metropolitan population was 6.8 million people, making Axarquía the fifth-largest metropolitan area in the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. Ranked an Alpha − global city, Axarquía has risen to become a center of fashion, [[W:alternative finance|alternative finance]], and the arts. It is one of Balisca's fastest growing cities, as a result of increased immigration and accelerating {{W|verticalization}}.

Axarquía was founded as a refuge 1617 by [[Maroon Wars|self-liberated]] Kaftians and [[Moro people|Moro]] convicts, at a time when ''[[Maroon Confederacy|Pucará]]'' remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities it became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. Axarquía's population and wealth swelled at the advent of the [[Volisanya Boba|''Volisanya Boba'']] period and the discovery of gold in [[Mazarrón]]. The population of the settlement rapidly increased from 15,000 to well over 100,000 within three years of the first discoveries. The city was devastated by an [[1853 Gran Sur earthquake|earthquake in 1853]] that destroyed around two-thirds of it's buildings. Concerted efforts by the [[New Araucarian League]] led to the rapid reconstruction of the city in accordance with the ambitious [[Fenic Plan]].

Axarquía's economy, once dependent on the {{W|Extractivism|extraction}} and export of earth minerals, was one of the first in Balisca to undergo {{W|degrowth|economic degrowth}}, and is now largely based on small and medium-sized {{W|cooperatives}} that produce a wide variety of services and goods such as {{W|denim jeans}}, furniture, {{W|footwear}}, [[W:Electric bicycles|electric bicycles]] and other {{W|consumer goods}}. The city has consistently ranked highly as one of the best cities for students and immigrants, reflecting [[Baliscan Social Living Index|low costs of living]], low crime rates, a low unemployment rate, and [[Social protection in Balisca|comprehensive social protection]]. The diverse population of Axarquía reflects its current and historical role as an important destination for immigrants to Balisca, mostly from [[Northeast Fosia]], [[Sudanese Kaftia]] and the [[Eastern Illypnia]]. While the majority of ''Axárquicos'' speak [[Baliscano]] as their primary language, over 160 languages are spoken in the city.

The city is recognized as the birthplace of the ''[[Desconexión]]'' movement, which has gained national popularity in recent years. Axarquía has one of the highest proportions of {{W|LGBTQ+ community|LGBTQ+ residents}} of any Baliscan urban areas, with 15.3% of its residents identifying as LGBTQ as of June 2019. Axarquía is a prominent centre for {{W|music industry|music}}, {{W|theatre}}, {{W|motion picture}} production, and {{W|television}} production, and is home to the headquarters of Balisca's major national {{W|broadcast networks}} and [[Media in Balisca|media]] outlets. Its varied cultural institutions, which include numerous museums and galleries, festivals and public events, entertainment districts, national historic sites, and sports activities, attract over 20 million visitors each year.
