== Demographics ==
{{Main|Demographics of Axarquía}}

{{See also|Demographics of Balisca}}Due to its history as an important [[Aethiopian Ocean|North Aethiopic]] port, Axarquía has always been one of the primary gateways into [[Balisca]]. This has attracted many immigrants and made the city a cosmopolitan {{W|melting pot}}. By the end of the 20th century about half the population originated from overseas (mostly from the [[Periphery countries]]).

It is the main component of an administrative area of Greater Axarquía, with a population of 3,218,071 in an area of {{convert|430.23|km2}} (density 7,300 inhabitants/km2). It is the central nucleus of the [[Axarquía metropolitan area]], which relies on a population of 6,892,427.

[[Baliscano]] is the most spoken language in Axarquía (according to the linguistic census held by the [[Juyú|Region of Juyú]] in 2022) and it is understood almost universally. [[Andalusian language|Andalusian]] also very commonly spoken in the city: it is fully understood by 25% of the population, while 32.3% can speak it, 59% can read it, and 43% can write it. Knowledge of [[TBA]] has increased significantly in recent decades thanks to a {{W|language immersion}} educational system.

In 1900, Axarquía had a population of 533,000, and it grew steadily until around 1950, when it started absorbing a high number of immigrants from overseas. Axarquía population stagnated in the 1970s as a result of the [[Special Period]]. After bottoming out in 2010 with 2,496,266 residents, the city's population began to rise again as younger people started to return, causing a great increase in housing prices.

In 1900, almost a third (28.9 percent) of the population were children (aged younger than 14 years). In 2017, this age group constituted only 12.7% of the population. In 2017, people aged between 15 and 24 years made up 9 percent of the population; those aged between 25 and 44 years made up 30.6 percent of the population; while those aged between 45 and 64 years formed 56.9% of all Axárquicos. In 1900, people aged 65 and older made up just 6.5 percent of the population. In 2017, this age group made up 18.5 percent of the population.
====Ethnography and migration ====
{{Main|Ethnography of Balisca}}

According to a 2021 [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística|ICS]] study, Axarquía was the most fourth-most ethnically diverse canton in Balisca among all communes of over one million people. In 2016, about 59% of the inhabitants of the city were born in Juyú and 18.5% had origins from other areas of the country. In addition to that, 33.9% of the population was born outside of Balisca, a proportion which has more than doubled since 2001 and more than quintupled since 1996 when it was 8.6 and 3.9% respectively. Of that, 49.5% were born in [[Abyala]], 23.5% in [[Fosia]], 21.8% in [[Kaftia]], 3.8% in [[Illypnia]], and 0.4% in [[Lurandia]]. Approximately 44.2% of the population over 5 years of age speak [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] at home; 23.8% speak [[Mozarabic]] at home. Also, 16.8% of the populace speak other [[w:Indo-European languages|Kemali-Illypnian languages]] at home. Another 13.5% speak an [[w:Languages of Asia|Fosian language]] at home.

Among the Fosian population, people of [[w:Chinese diaspora|Chinese ethnicity]] make up the largest ethnic group at 10.2% of Axarquía's population, with about 319,544 people; the other East and Northeast Fosian groups are: [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese]] (2.9%), [[Filipino Baliscans|Maharlikan]]s (1.7%), [[Kwangju Baliscans|Kwangju]] (0.3%), [[Darasian Baliscans|Darasians]] (0.2%), [[Vietnamese Baliscans|Balausians]] (0.2%), and [[Nusarayan Baliscans|Nusarayans]] and "[[Burmese Baliscans|Burmese]]" both make up 0.1% of the population. People of [[North Fosia|North Fosian]] descent make up 7.8% of Axarquía's population: [[Kemali Baliscans|Kemali]] (5.3%), [[Mugladesh|Mugladeshi]] (1.5%), "[[Pakistani Baliscans|Pakistani]]s" (0.7%), and "[[Nepalese Baliscans|Nepali]]" (0.2%).

